  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nOngoing Fog & Increasing Storm Potential:\n\nLight southwest winds continue through the early morning, gradually\nincreasing dewpoint temperatures into the low to mid 60s.\nConcurrent cooling of surface temperatures into the low to mid \n60s is resulting in fog formation primarily locally in \nnortheast Iowa. Additional, more widespread impacts from FG have\nbeen stifled by off surface winds of 10-20 mph on local VWPs. \nTherefore, while Patchy FG is evident on satellite imagery, \nimpacts have remained minimal. Any fog that does form will \ndissipate by the mid- morning.\n\nA mid-upper level wave begins to take shape during the mid- \nmorning hours through central Minnesota, reaching the local\nforecast area by the early afternoon, eventually exiting through\nthe evening. Recent Convective Allowing Model (CAM) dProg/dt has\nbeen exhibiting a MCS resemblant feature developing with said\nwave. An initial capping inversion will limit initial \ninstability, 1000 to 2000 J/kg of MLCAPE, during the morning. As\nthis MCS moves further into our CWA, instability gradually \nincreases as the CAP decreases. This could enhance the damaging\nwind gust threat as the DCAPE values grow as we get into the \nafternoon, still not as favorable as they could be with values \naround 700 to 850 J/kg from various CAMs. The bulk shear also \nincreases as the wave moves through with values of 40 to 50 kts.\n\nThe main threat with this system would be damaging winds, but \nsince this is a northwest flow regime, this storm complex will \nlikely wobble a bit in the model guidance before settling on a \ntrack. Storms may also initialize along the front further north \nwhere better forcing is at. Any storms that form along the front\nwould be discrete at a first, but then form into lines or \nclusters rather quickly due to the messy 3 to 6km shear profiles\nin the hodographs. Overall, while confidence in storms is \nincreasing, the exact location of both this complex and any \nafternoon storms remain uncertain. \n\nSaturday Night - Friday: Periodic Storm Chances\n\nQuasi-NW/zonal flow remains the dominant flow pattern in both \nensemble and deterministic guidance over the coming week. With the \ncold front passage today, this will shift storm chances further to \nour south for Saturday. By Saturday night a low forms in the Central \nPlains which in turn shifts the baroclinic zone back north over \nportions of our forecast area for both Sunday and Monday. There \nstill remains quite a bit of uncertainty regarding how storms will \nform and where exactly they form. The GFS has the boundary further \ninto our area compared to the CMC and ECMWF which keep it further \ninto central Iowa for Saturday night then bring it back north for \nSunday into Monday. Given that we will be in the aforementioned \nnorthwest flow pattern, any perturbation could be enough to cause \nstorms to form whether along a boundary or become a MCS and move \nthrough the area. These mesoscale details are tough to pin down this \nfar out and are directly impacted by the previous day's convection. \n\nBy midweek, guidance has been in decent agreement that this boundary \nshifts south of the local area keeping the majority of the \nprecipitation chances south of the CWA. Similar to the last few \ndiscussions, with good agreement on the flow pattern and daily storm \nchances, high temperatures are likely to stay in the normal to \nslightly below normal range with highs in the mid 70s to mid\n80s.",
